I would be inclined to agree that it does not reflect the developers'
true intent; my suspicion is that one of the developers wanted to create a
random number generation method with a certain level of obfuscation, to
attempt to prevent people from simply praying every 351 turns.

They succeeded in part, but as can be seen, if you have the source code
in hand just making the math a bit harder doesn't actually stop anyone
from figuring out the optimal solution.  If the above was their intent,
they also failed by not graphing out the distribution prior to actually
implementing it; as mentioned in the prior message, the chances of
getting anything past 700 turns are so trivial that it might as well
have been rnd(700) for all the difference it makes.

For people who have difficulty with probability perspective, I suggest
playing some backgammon.  An educational game in many ways.

